[
    {
        "content": "<p>I noticed that we have a notation <code>![1, 2]</code> for <code>Fin 2 → ℝ</code>. In mathlib, we have notation </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Finsupp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Notation</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Real</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I wonder whether we have alternative notation to express a <code>Finsupp</code> function <code>Fin 2 →₀ ℝ</code>. I feel that the notation above is not that convenient. I would expect  something like <code>fun₀ ![1, 2]</code>. Thanks in advance for your help.</p>",
        "id": 608686749,
        "sender_full_name": "Wenrong Zou",
        "timestamp": 1783423531
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or maybe something like <code>![1, 2]₀</code>. I am not sure it is useful or possible to add it.</p>",
        "id": 608687286,
        "sender_full_name": "Wenrong Zou",
        "timestamp": 1783423707
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>An alternative is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finsupp.equivFunOnFinite#doc\">docs#Finsupp.equivFunOnFinite</a>, so you'd write <code>Finsupp.equivFunOnFinite.symm ![1, 2]</code>. (It is a very long name and I've been annoyed by this before.)</p>",
        "id": 608687310,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1783423715
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, you are right. But there are too many letters. :(</p>",
        "id": 608687447,
        "sender_full_name": "Wenrong Zou",
        "timestamp": 1783423767
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I do want to change the implementation of <code>![...]</code> notation in the near future though, to replace <code>Fin.cons 1 (Fin.cons 2 Fin.emptyElim)</code> with <code>Array.get #[1, 2]</code>. Maybe we could also add a Finsupp version at the same time?</p>",
        "id": 608687772,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1783423876
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Great! Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 608687860,
        "sender_full_name": "Wenrong Zou",
        "timestamp": 1783423910
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"641254\">Wenrong Zou</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Notation.20for.20.60Finsupp.60/near/608687286\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Or maybe something like <code>![1, 2]₀</code>. I am not sure it is useful or possible to add it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think <code>!₀[1, 2]</code> is better.</p>",
        "id": 608748825,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1783439490
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's already taken for the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> norm, I think</p>",
        "id": 608790392,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1783443551
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>though there wouldn't be a conflict if that notation for tuples w/ <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> norm used a superscript :^)<br>\n(perhaps it uses subscripts because they have a shorter abbreviation in VSCode?)</p>",
        "id": 609334048,
        "sender_full_name": "Albert Smith",
        "timestamp": 1783635806
    }
]